{"name":"Highlands Moss","hex":"#445500","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"highlands","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A high area of land, lands higher than surrounding areas."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/highlands"},{"term":"moss","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various small, green, seedless plants growing on the ground or on the surfaces of trees, stones, etc.; now specifically, a plant of the phylum Bryophyta (formerly division Musci).","A kind or species of such plants."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To become covered with moss.","To cover (something) with moss."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moss"}],"entries":[{"title":"Moss","blocks":[{"text":"Mosses are small, non-vascular flowerless plants in the taxonomic division Bryophyta sensu stricto. Bryophyta sensu lato may also refer to the parent group, bryophytes, which comprises liverworts, mosses, and hornworts. Mosses typically form dense green clumps or mats, often in damp or shady locations. The individual plants are usually composed of simple leaves that are generally only one cell thick, attached to a stem that may be branched or unbranched and has only a limited role in conducting water and nutrients. Although some species have conducting tissues, these are generally poorly developed and structurally different from similar tissue found in vascular plants. Mosses do not have seeds and after fertilisation develop sporophytes with unbranched stalks topped with single capsules containing spores. They are typically 0.2–10 cm (0.1–3.9 in) tall, though some species are much larger. Dawsonia superba, the tallest moss in the world, can grow to 60 cm (24 in) in height. There are approximately 12,000 species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Tionesta-ac-moss2.jpg/330px-Tionesta-ac-moss2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moss","wikidata":"Q25347"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Bryum_capillare_leaf_cells.jpg/500px-Bryum_capillare_leaf_cells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Bryum_capillare_leaf_cells.jpg/1280px-Bryum_capillare_leaf_cells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Bryum_capillare_leaf_cells.jpg/500px-Bryum_capillare_leaf_cells.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chloroplasts (green discs) and accumulated starch granules in cells of Rosulabryum capillare","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABryum_capillare_leaf_cells.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Moss_leaf_under_microscope.jpg/500px-Moss_leaf_under_microscope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Moss_leaf_under_microscope.jpg/1280px-Moss_leaf_under_microscope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Moss_leaf_under_microscope.jpg/500px-Moss_leaf_under_microscope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Moss leaf under microscope, showing gemmae and a hair point (40×)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoss_leaf_under_microscope.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Moss_Gametophytes_Sporophytes.JPG/500px-Moss_Gametophytes_Sporophytes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Moss_Gametophytes_Sporophytes.JPG/1280px-Moss_Gametophytes_Sporophytes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=p